{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Throwaway exploration has the lowest possible cost of being wrong: the code is deleted or rewritten before it matters, so judging it only by whether it works is the fastest way to learn. Full vibe coding keeps experiments cheap and lets you test many ideas before committing to one."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo to stakeholders or investors is meant to be seen, not maintained, but a visible failure in front of an audience is expensive. Let AI write most of it, review selectively, and lean on tests to confirm the demo path actually works end to end."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Real early users depend on it and their feedback shapes the product, so code you don't understand becomes a liability you inherit. Line-by-line review of every AI change keeps you able to debug, iterate, and refactor as usage grows, while still gaining AI's speed."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team mean failures have real cost and code is read far more often than written. Every AI change should be reviewed at least line by line, with tests and standards enforced in review, so the team owns what ships."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems carry legal, financial, and human risk, and demand traceable reasoning that AI-generated code alone cannot yet provide. Use AI to suggest, but have a person write or rewrite and take ownership of every line, backed by rigorous verification."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Recommended vibe-coding level decreases as the cost of being wrong rises: prototype 4, proof-of-concept 3, mvp 2, production 2, critical 1. Full vibe coding is fine only for throwaway work; once real users or money or safety are involved, a person must read and own the code. The guiding rule: let AI write the less the code matters for longer, and always keep a human accountable for anything you have to maintain or defend."
}
